Under Article 39(1) of the federal data protection act, a federal body may process personal data for non-personal purposes such as research, planning or statistics only if all of the following are met:

a. the data are anonymised as soon as the processing purpose allows;
b. when disclosing particularly sensitive personal data to private persons, the body does so only in a way that the data subjects cannot be identified;
c. the recipient passes the data on to third parties only with the consent of the federal body that disclosed them; and
d. results are published only in a way that the data subjects cannot be identified.

Under Article 39(2), Article 6(3), Article 34(2) and Article 36(1) do not apply to this processing. The passages do not say anything further about the private institute's own obligations or about conditions for disclosure beyond these points.
